    "texte": [
      "CRITICAL ESSAY ON ORGANISATIONAL SOCIOLOGY AND CORPORATE POWER — EUROPEAN JOURNAL OF EXECUTIVE GOVERNANCE. While contemporary management rhetoric routinely celebrates horizontal collaboration, employee empowerment, and participatory leadership, an empirical examination of corporate power structures reveals a marked counter-trend: the hyper-concentration of strategic decision-making authority within an increasingly narrow executive apex. Across major technology conglomerates, financial institutions, and industrial groups, the rise of the 'imperial chief executive'—often fortified by dual-class voting share structures—has fundamentally altered the internal balance of institutional power.",
      "This structural centralisation is most visibly exemplified by the proliferation of founder-controlled dual-class equity arrangements, wherein super-voting shares confer ten or twenty votes per share upon a handful of insiders while public institutional investors hold non-voting or single-vote stock. By insulating executive leadership permanently from shareholder proxy contests and hostile takeover discipline, such governance architectures dismantle the traditional checks and balances designed to restrain unchecked managerial discretion.",
      "Within the internal hierarchy of the enterprise, the concentration of authority at the apex triggers a pervasive culture of upward deference and self-censorship. As sociologist Robert Michels famously articulated through his 'iron law of oligarchy', any large-scale organisation tends inexorably toward rule by a self-perpetuating elite that monopolises strategic information, controls agenda-setting mechanisms, and frames institutional priorities in ways that render dissent appear disloyal or technically naive.",
      "The operational consequences of unbridled executive centralisation become acutely visible during high-stakes strategic pivots or capital allocation failures. When a dominant chief executive simultaneously chairs the board of directors and hand-picks compliant committee chairs, dissenting operational warnings from middle management and risk officers are systematically filtered out before reaching the boardroom. Deprived of vigorous internal debate, the enterprise becomes dangerously vulnerable to confirmation bias and catastrophic strategic overreach.",
      "Furthermore, the erosion of internal organisational democracy undermines the psychological contract binding skilled professionals to the firm. Knowledge workers, engineers, and domain specialists recruited for their analytical autonomy rapidly become disillusioned when critical decisions affecting product ethics, technological deployment, or workforce restructuring are handed down as opaque executive edicts without meaningful consultation or employee voice mechanisms.",
      "Proponents of concentrated executive authority frequently argue that decisive, centralised leadership is indispensable for speed of execution in hyper-competitive markets, contending that deliberative governance breeds bureaucratic paralysis. Yet longitudinal comparative studies demonstrate that while autocratic decision-making may accelerate initial action in stable environments, it severely degrades decision quality in complex, uncertain conditions where distributed local intelligence is essential to detect emerging operational anomalies.",
      "Restoring a healthy equilibrium between executive decisiveness and organisational democracy requires deliberate institutional redesign rather than mere leadership pledges. Effective governance models separate the roles of board chair and chief executive, institute works councils or employee board representation with genuine consultative rights, and establish protected internal channels through which technical specialists can escalate substantive objections directly to independent board committees.",
      "Ultimately, an enterprise cannot sustain long-term intellectual vitality if its internal governance mirrors an enlightened autocracy. True organisational resilience emerges when strategic authority is anchored in transparent deliberation, institutionalised dissent, and shared accountability across all tiers of the enterprise."
